WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:03.790
Welcome to this Java tutorial, where you'll use

00:00:03.790 --> 00:00:07.047
an array, a fixed sequence of values, select its

00:00:07.047 --> 00:00:10.101
elements by index, and traverse that sequence

00:00:10.101 --> 00:00:12.273
with a loop to produce a report.

00:00:12.929 --> 00:00:16.133
Storing an ordered batch and applying one rule

00:00:16.133 --> 00:00:18.989
to its entries helps applications process

00:00:18.989 --> 00:00:21.914
measurements, report inventory, and handle

00:00:21.914 --> 00:00:25.118
repeated work without a separate statement for

00:00:25.118 --> 00:00:25.954
every value.

00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:29.678
A campus tour coordinator needs a route report

00:00:29.678 --> 00:00:31.990
from a supplied list of stop names.

00:00:31.990 --> 00:00:35.160
We'll store those names in their visiting order,

00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:38.065
replace one stop when the route changes, and

00:00:38.065 --> 00:00:40.311
print each stop with its position.

00:00:40.311 --> 00:00:43.415
Replacement must keep the same number of stops.

00:00:44.069 --> 00:00:46.768
A useful report should preserve the route's

00:00:46.768 --> 00:00:49.530
order, include the replacement, and show the

00:00:49.530 --> 00:00:50.284
entry count.

00:00:50.284 --> 00:00:53.234
We'll first work through a two-stop route, then

00:00:53.234 --> 00:00:56.059
use what we've learned to predict a different

00:00:56.059 --> 00:00:58.319
three-stop report before running it.

00:00:58.394 --> 00:01:01.714
Let's open Main.java in the Workspace and build

00:01:01.714 --> 00:01:02.844
the first route.

00:01:06.912 --> 00:01:09.942
Main is the class containing our code, and its

00:01:09.942 --> 00:01:12.643
main method is where this program starts.

00:01:12.643 --> 00:01:15.409
Inside that method, we'll keep library and

00:01:15.409 --> 00:01:17.649
studio together in a String array.

00:01:17.649 --> 00:01:19.757
Each stored value is an element.

00:01:19.757 --> 00:01:22.524
One array keeps the route's visiting order

00:01:22.524 --> 00:01:25.620
explicit instead of scattering its stops across

00:01:25.620 --> 00:01:26.937
unrelated variables.

00:01:39.798 --> 00:01:42.898
The square brackets in the type tell Java that

00:01:42.898 --> 00:01:45.325
route refers to an array of Strings.

00:01:45.325 --> 00:01:48.155
The initializer, the values inside braces,

00:01:48.155 --> 00:01:50.514
creates two elements in that order.

00:01:50.514 --> 00:01:53.547
Their indices are zero and one: an index is a

00:01:53.547 --> 00:01:56.311
position number, and Java starts counting

00:01:56.311 --> 00:01:57.524
positions at zero.

00:01:57.524 --> 00:02:00.759
The array's length is two, because length counts

00:02:00.759 --> 00:02:01.298
entries.

00:02:01.324 --> 00:02:04.583
The coordinator has changed the second stop to

00:02:04.583 --> 00:02:05.150
gallery.

00:02:05.150 --> 00:02:08.268
We need to replace the element at index one.

00:02:08.268 --> 00:02:11.456
Assigning to that position changes its stored

00:02:11.456 --> 00:02:14.432
value while keeping the first stop and the

00:02:14.432 --> 00:02:16.274
two-position array intact.

00:02:19.837 --> 00:02:22.203
The second position now holds gallery.

00:02:22.203 --> 00:02:25.192
Writing separate print statements would work for

00:02:25.192 --> 00:02:27.745
this tiny route, but a longer route would

00:02:27.745 --> 00:02:29.426
require more repeated code.

00:02:29.426 --> 00:02:32.291
Traversal means visiting the elements in turn.

00:02:32.291 --> 00:02:35.280
We'll use the for-loop pattern you already know,

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:38.144
starting at zero and continuing only while the

00:02:38.144 --> 00:02:40.012
index is less than the length.

00:02:53.251 --> 00:02:55.860
Each accepted index selects one stop for the

00:02:55.860 --> 00:02:56.809
print statement.

00:02:56.809 --> 00:02:59.537
The update then advances to the next position.

00:02:59.537 --> 00:03:02.206
With two entries, zero and one are valid, but

00:03:02.206 --> 00:03:02.859
two is not.

00:03:02.859 --> 00:03:05.112
When the index reaches two, the strict

00:03:05.112 --> 00:03:07.781
comparison is false, so the loop stops before

00:03:07.781 --> 00:03:09.501
attempting an invalid access.

00:03:09.501 --> 00:03:12.051
We'll add the entry count after the loop to

00:03:12.051 --> 00:03:14.601
distinguish that count from the last index.

00:03:20.126 --> 00:03:23.438
For this worked route, we expect index zero to

00:03:23.438 --> 00:03:26.821
report library and index one to report gallery,

00:03:26.821 --> 00:03:29.269
followed by an entry count of two.

00:03:29.269 --> 00:03:32.724
Let's run the program and compare that reasoning

00:03:32.724 --> 00:03:33.876
with its output.

00:03:38.326 --> 00:03:41.098
The report shows library, then gallery, with

00:03:41.098 --> 00:03:42.610
Entries: two underneath.

00:03:42.610 --> 00:03:45.509
Replacement changed a value, not the length or

00:03:45.509 --> 00:03:45.887
order.

00:03:45.887 --> 00:03:48.848
The loop printed exactly two stop lines because

00:03:48.848 --> 00:03:51.620
its condition accepted only indices zero and

00:03:51.620 --> 00:03:51.872
one.

00:03:51.872 --> 00:03:54.897
Notice that the array length has no parentheses;

00:03:54.897 --> 00:03:57.606
it is a field holding the count, unlike the

00:03:57.606 --> 00:04:00.126
String length method you've used before.

00:04:00.203 --> 00:04:03.301
Traversal can also combine values into a result.

00:04:03.301 --> 00:04:06.335
A stockroom worker has counted two units in one

00:04:06.335 --> 00:04:09.111
bin and four in another and needs the total

00:04:09.111 --> 00:04:10.144
number of units.

00:04:10.144 --> 00:04:13.178
We don't need position labels for this task, so

00:04:13.178 --> 00:04:16.212
we'll replace the route example with an integer

00:04:16.212 --> 00:04:19.181
array and an enhanced for loop, which supplies

00:04:19.181 --> 00:04:21.053
each element's value in turn.

00:04:30.346 --> 00:04:33.384
Amounts stores units per bin, and total starts

00:04:33.384 --> 00:04:36.291
at zero because no bins have been processed.

00:04:36.291 --> 00:04:39.329
The enhanced loop will supply each amount to a

00:04:39.329 --> 00:04:40.320
local variable.

00:04:40.320 --> 00:04:43.491
Adding that value to the earlier total preserves

00:04:43.491 --> 00:04:45.208
the units already counted.

00:04:45.208 --> 00:04:48.247
This produces a running total: the accumulated

00:04:48.247 --> 00:04:51.021
result after the entries processed so far.

00:05:02.341 --> 00:05:05.062
The first iteration adds two to zero.

00:05:05.062 --> 00:05:08.593
The second adds four to the retained two, making

00:05:08.593 --> 00:05:09.328
six units.

00:05:09.328 --> 00:05:12.859
We print once after the loop, so this is a final

00:05:12.859 --> 00:05:15.654
total rather than a line for each bin.

00:05:15.654 --> 00:05:17.566
Let's run the stock check.

00:05:21.906 --> 00:05:24.293
Units: six is the combined quantity.

00:05:24.293 --> 00:05:27.012
It is not the array length: there are two

00:05:27.012 --> 00:05:29.200
entries, each representing a bin.

00:05:29.200 --> 00:05:32.052
The enhanced loop was convenient because we

00:05:32.052 --> 00:05:34.439
needed each value but not its index.

00:05:34.439 --> 00:05:37.622
The indexed loop served the route report because

00:05:37.622 --> 00:05:39.545
its position labels mattered.

00:05:39.545 --> 00:05:42.131
Both forms visit the elements in order.

00:05:42.233 --> 00:05:45.331
Let's return to the tour coordinator with the

00:05:45.331 --> 00:05:47.122
lesson's three-stop route.

00:05:47.122 --> 00:05:50.358
We'll replace the stock example with desk, lab,

00:05:50.358 --> 00:05:53.456
and hall, change one stored stop, and use the

00:05:53.456 --> 00:05:55.246
indexed reporting pattern.

00:05:55.246 --> 00:05:58.551
This time, you'll trace the result before we run

00:05:58.551 --> 00:05:58.758
it.

00:06:09.305 --> 00:06:12.596
The initializer supplies three names in visiting

00:06:12.596 --> 00:06:13.008
order.

00:06:13.008 --> 00:06:16.025
We'll assign studio to index one, then print

00:06:16.025 --> 00:06:19.110
each valid index with the element it selects.

00:06:19.110 --> 00:06:21.853
The final line reports the array length.

00:06:21.853 --> 00:06:25.076
Apply the distinction between a position and an

00:06:25.076 --> 00:06:28.230
entry count as you read the completed program.

00:06:51.103 --> 00:06:54.226
Before we run, write every line you expect,

00:06:54.226 --> 00:06:57.567
including the position labels and final count.

00:06:57.567 --> 00:07:00.762
Identify which original stop is replaced and

00:07:00.762 --> 00:07:04.030
explain why the loop never reads index three.

00:07:04.030 --> 00:07:07.153
Pause the video here if you need more time.

00:07:15.221 --> 00:07:18.154
Let's click Run and compare the actual route

00:07:18.154 --> 00:07:20.021
report with your prediction.

00:07:24.470 --> 00:07:27.359
The report shows desk at zero, studio at one,

00:07:27.359 --> 00:07:30.184
and hall at two, followed by Entries: three.

00:07:30.184 --> 00:07:32.816
The assignment replaced lab at index one.

00:07:32.816 --> 00:07:35.513
The other entries stayed in place, and the

00:07:35.513 --> 00:07:36.797
length stayed three.

00:07:36.797 --> 00:07:39.879
After printing hall, the loop advanced to three,

00:07:39.879 --> 00:07:42.896
where the strict bound became false and stopped

00:07:42.896 --> 00:07:43.795
the traversal.

00:07:44.066 --> 00:07:47.042
We've used an array to keep a fixed sequence, an

00:07:47.042 --> 00:07:49.645
index to replace and read one element, and

00:07:49.645 --> 00:07:52.187
traversal to report every valid position.

00:07:52.187 --> 00:07:54.852
We also used an enhanced loop and a running

00:07:54.852 --> 00:07:56.588
total to combine quantities.

00:07:56.588 --> 00:07:59.068
The route report meets the coordinator's

00:07:59.068 --> 00:08:01.981
requirements, while the stock example shows how

00:08:01.981 --> 00:08:04.957
the same ordered processing supports a different

00:08:04.957 --> 00:08:05.391
result.

00:08:06.679 --> 00:08:09.809
In the notebook, practice choosing valid indices

00:08:09.809 --> 00:08:12.939
and explaining what each loop's result measures.

00:08:12.939 --> 00:08:15.938
Compare the array's length with its last valid

00:08:15.938 --> 00:08:18.938
index, and the number of entries with a sum of

00:08:18.938 --> 00:08:19.785
their values.

00:08:19.785 --> 00:08:22.915
Those distinctions will help you choose and test

00:08:22.915 --> 00:08:25.654
the collections used later in this course.
